{% extends 'blog/post.html.twig' %}
{% block article %}
    <article id="blog-post">
        <h1>Flow PHP - Release 0.45.0</h1>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            Flow 0.45.0 is probably the biggest change in Flow internals so far. Rows are gone, at least the way they
            used to exist, and Flow got a real query planner.
        </p>
        <p>
            Most of pipelines are going to work exactly as before, just faster and with less memory, but there are
            a few things to check before updating, so please read this one to the end.
        </p>

        <h2>Columnar memory layout</h2>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            Since the very first version, Flow was keeping data as rows. Each <code>Row</code> was an object holding
            a collection of entries, and each entry was another object. It is a simple model, easy to reason about, but
            it comes with a price. Millions of small objects are expensive to create, to keep in memory and to iterate over.
        </p>
        <p>
            In 0.45.0 the <code>Row</code> is removed. A batch (<code>Rows</code>) now stores one column per schema definition,
            and every reader, writer, function and processor works on columns.
        </p>
        <p>
            This is how DuckDB, Polars or Apache Arrow keep the data, and there are good reasons for it.
            A function that adds two columns goes through two arrays of numbers, not through millions of objects.
        </p>
        <h3>Column Backend</h3>
        <p>
            Where those columns live is decided by a backend, and a data frame has exactly one, set on its config.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>PhpBackend</code> - columns are PHP arrays, it needs nothing and it is the reference implementation</li>
            <li><code>RustBackend</code> - columns are Apache Arrow arrays, it comes with the <code>flow_php</code> extension</li>
            <li><code>AdaptiveBackend</code> - the default one, picks <code>RustBackend</code> when the extension is loaded and <code>PhpBackend</code> when it is not</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Both backends give the same values, the extension is just an accelerator. With it, CSV, JSON and Parquet are
            read and written as native columns, without going through PHP values at all.
        </p>
        <p>
            You can read more about it in <a href="/documentation/components/core/column-backend">documentation</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Query planner</h2>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            Before this release, a pipeline was just a list of steps executed one after another, exactly in the order they were written.
            Whatever was written, was executed, even when there was a much cheaper way to get the same result.
        </p>
        <p>
            Now Flow works like a database does. A pipeline is first described, then optimized, and only then executed:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Logical plan</strong> - each DataFrame method adds a node to a plan, nothing reads the data yet</li>
            <li><strong>Optimizer</strong> - rewrites the plan into a cheaper one that gives the same result</li>
            <li><strong>Planner</strong> - turns the plan into pipelines, picks the algorithms and their settings</li>
            <li><strong>Executor</strong> - pulls batches from the extractor, runs them through the steps and hands them to the loaders</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Each node of the plan declares what it does to the data: how many rows it can return, if it changes them,
            if it needs all of them at once and which columns it redefines. That is what the optimizer reads to decide
            if a rewrite is safe.
        </p>
        <h3>Optimizer</h3>
        <p>
            The optimizer is a list of rules, and each rule knows one way to make a plan cheaper.
            0.45.0 comes with the first five:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>CombineLimits</code> - two <code>limit()</code> calls one after another become one, with the smaller limit</li>
            <li><code>CombineSortAndLimit</code> - <code>sortBy()</code> followed by <code>limit()</code> keeps only the top rows instead of sorting everything</li>
            <li><code>PushLimitIntoSource</code> - the extractor stops reading once the limit is reached</li>
            <li><code>PushFilterIntoSource</code> - a <code>filter()</code> on partition columns skips whole partition directories</li>
            <li><code>CountFromStatistics</code> - <code>count()</code> over a source that knows its rows exactly reads that number, and no rows</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The second one is a good example of what it gives. Take a pipeline that needs 10 biggest orders:
        </p>
        <pre><code class="language-php" {{ stimulus_controller('syntax_highlight') }}>{% apply escape %}{% include template_folder ~ '/top_n.php' %}{% endapply %}</code></pre>
        <p>
            Without the optimizer, Flow would sort the whole file, for a big one spilling to disk on the way, just to throw
            away everything except 10 rows. With it, the sort and the limit become one top N step that keeps only the 10 best rows.
            The code of the pipeline stays exactly the same.
        </p>
        <p>
            Rules can be turned off one by one, and the optimizer can be extended with your own rules through
            <code>config_builder()->optimizer()</code>. It is also possible to run a pipeline with no rewrites at all,
            which is pretty useful when something needs to be debugged.
        </p>
        <p>
            This is just a beginning. With a plan and an optimizer in place, each next rule is a small addition,
            not a rewrite of the engine.
        </p>
        <h3>Explain</h3>
        <p>
            To see what Flow is going to do, use <code>explain()</code>. It answers from the plan, without reading a single row.
        </p>
        <pre><code class="language-php" {{ stimulus_controller('syntax_highlight') }}>{% apply escape %}{% include template_folder ~ '/explain.php' %}{% endapply %}</code></pre>
        <pre><code class="language-text">{% apply escape %}{% include template_folder ~ '/explain.txt' %}{% endapply %}</code></pre>
        <p>
            The plan is a tree read from the bottom up, the source is always <code>#1</code>.
            In the example above the filter is used by two consumers, so it is printed once and the second one points back at it.
        </p>
        <p>
            It can be printed at three stages: as it was written, after the optimizer, and as a physical plan with pipelines,
            algorithms and their settings. There are also four formats to pick from: tree, flow, boxes and declarations.
        </p>

        <h2>Source statistics</h2>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            A planner without any knowledge about the data cannot do much. That is why every extractor now declares what it
            knows about its source before reading it, how many rows and how many bytes.
        </p>
        <p>
            Parquet and Floe take exact numbers from file footers, CSV and JSON Lines estimate rows from the sampled row size,
            PostgreSQL and Doctrine adapters ask the database through <code>EXPLAIN</code>.
        </p>
        <pre><code class="language-php" {{ stimulus_controller('syntax_highlight') }}>{% apply escape %}{% include template_folder ~ '/count.php' %}{% endapply %}</code></pre>
        <p>
            The first thing that benefits from it is <code>count()</code>. When the statistics are exact, it answers without reading rows.
        </p>
        <p>
            There is also <code>analyze()->withSourceStatistics()</code> that reports declared vs. read rows per source,
            and <code>--stats-sources</code> option in CLI.
        </p>

        <h2>Native readers</h2>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            The <code>flow_php</code> extension got a native CSV tokenizer, reader and schema inference. When the extension is loaded,
            CSV adapter is using them automatically.
        </p>
        <p>
            Floe frames are decoded straight into Rows and encoded straight from Rows in one native pass, and casting ISO datetimes
            with <code>Z</code> suffix is around 9 times faster in PHP and 14 times faster in the native hydrator.
        </p>

        <h2>Time zones</h2>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            Datetime handling was pretty loose before. One column could hold values in different time zones, and casting a string
            without a zone was using whatever <code>date.timezone</code> was set to.
        </p>
        <p>
            Now a datetime column carries exactly one time zone, UTC by default. It can be changed through <code>type_datetime($zone)</code>
            or <code>datetime_schema(zone:)</code>, and every cast lands in the zone of its column.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>naive strings ignore <code>date.timezone</code></li>
            <li>date casts land at midnight UTC</li>
            <li>merging datetime columns of different zones widens to UTC</li>
            <li>date and datetime keys at the same instant join and group together</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Transactions</h2>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            Writes got <code>Sink</code> and <code>Transaction</code> contracts, so a write can fan out or commit per batch.
            On top of it there are <code>DbalTransaction</code> and <code>PostgreSqlTransaction</code>, both with a configurable isolation level.
        </p>

        <h2>Parquet</h2>
        <hr/>
        <ul>
            <li><code>DECIMAL</code> on <code>INT32</code>, <code>INT64</code>, <code>BYTE_ARRAY</code> and <code>FLBA</code> of any width</li>
            <li><code>TIMESTAMP</code> and <code>TIME</code> honour <code>MILLIS</code>, <code>MICROS</code> and <code>NANOS</code> units</li>
            <li><code>DELTA_BINARY_PACKED</code>, unsigned integers and <code>INT96</code> follow the spec</li>
            <li>arrow-ext exchanges batches with <code>flow_php</code> through the Arrow C Data Interface</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Other changes</h2>
        <hr/>
        <ul>
            <li><code>DataFrame::forEach()</code>, <code>addRowIndex()</code>, <code>aggregateBy()</code>, <code>asExtractor()</code> and <code>isRepeatable()</code></li>
            <li>PostgreSQL: <code>COLLATE</code> support in the query builder, <code>Index::toSql()</code> and <code>Trigger::toSql()</code></li>
            <li>CLI: <code>ProgressBarLoader</code> advancing a progress bar per batch</li>
            <li>A mismatched <code>flow_php</code> or arrow extension build is refused with a clear message</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Heads up</h2>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            A change that big cannot come without breaking something:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>Row</code> is removed, custom extractors, loaders and functions need to work on columns</li>
            <li>Existing <code>.floe</code> files must be rewritten, the footer has a new format</li>
            <li><code>DataFrame::run()</code> no longer takes a callback, use <code>forEach()</code></li>
            <li><code>Config::optimizer()</code> was replaced by <code>planner()</code> and <code>executor()</code></li>
            <li>A union other than <code>null|T</code> inside a column type throws <code>UnsupportedUnionTypeException</code></li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Please check the <a href="/documentation/upgrading">upgrade guide</a> before updating.
        </p>
        <p>
            Those are just the most important additions to Flow PHP. For more details, check full at <a href="/changelog">Changelog</a>
        </p>
    </article>
{% endblock %}
